WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.240
There’s this piece from search for the Holy
Grail. And the Holy Grail is a myth that was

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.300
constructed in England. And-And the myth goes
something like this:

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:47.089
There is a-- There is a cup, the Grail, used
to hold Christ’s blood. And that cup has

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:51.010
a-- has redemptive significance and it’s
been lost.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.199
And the knights – King Arthur’s knights
– who go off to look for the Holy Grail

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:56.859
are after this cup. So, it’s a redemption
story, right?

00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:03.850
It means, the world’s damned, unredeemed.
There’s some object that can serve as, as

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:08.330
the source of redemption – the source of
nourishment, say, thinking about it from a

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.550
symbolic perspective – and it’s worthwhile
to go on a quest of that sort.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.150
And the King Arthur story is set up in an
interesting way, ‘cause there’s a king,

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.860
Arthur, but he has all these knights, these
nobles, and they all sit at a round table.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.070
And they’re at a round table because they’re
equals. So, although it’s a hierarchical

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:28.900
story, there’s-there’s a motif in it that
transcends the hierarchy.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:35.480
It says: Well… yeah, under normal circumstances
everyone’s arranged in a hierarchy. But

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.630
when you’re out to seek whatever you need,
then everyone’s an equal. And so, fine,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.240
so they sit at the round table, and then they
go off to search for the Holy Grail

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.870
And the story opens with a very interesting
motif, which is the knights look at the forest

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:53.100
and then they try to find the part that looks
the darkest, to them. And then, they go that

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.000
way. That’s the marker for their mission,
right, to go to the darkest place. And of

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.610
course, each knight goes off in a different
direction because the world looks slightly

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:06.490
different to each knight.
So… objectively speaking, they’re going

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.100
to a different place. But psychologically
speaking, they are going to the same place,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.030
right? And that place, I suppose, is being
represented in mythology and literature as

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.720
“The Heart of Darkness”.
And if you were ever curious about why people

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.290
aren’t enlightened – since it seems to
be a possibility – you can always think

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.750
about the story of King Arthur and the knights
of the Holy Grail, and think: Well, do you

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.030
really want to enter the forest at the darkest
place? And the answer to that is of course:

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:37.280
No, because the darkest place means precisely
that place you least want to go. And it’s

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.280
the same for everyone
So then, I have this little nephew, although

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:50.380
he’s almost 15 now. He had this dream when
he was four years old. And the background

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:55.330
to the dream is this: He was waking up in
the middle of the night for months, screaming.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:01.300
He had night terrors. And this went on for
like six months. And what was happening in

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.510
his life was twofold: There was some instability
in his family, ‘cause his parents got divorced

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.130
about a year after that. And also, he was
at the transition point from staying at home

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.430
to going to kindergarten.
So, you know, not only was he making the big

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.319
move out there into the terrible world, but
the stable point from which he might like

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:22.930
to have moved was shaky. So, you know, he
wasn’t having that great a time.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.240
so anyways he’s screaming away at night,
and this is pretty unsettling, right, ‘cause

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.770
night terrors are no joke, and so he’s upset
about it, and his mom’s upset about it,

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.910
and so I’m watching him and he’s running
around the house; he’s only about this high,

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:38.810
a very verbal kid, and he’s got this knight
hat on, and this sword and this shield, and

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.130
he’s running around the house being a knight,
and at night he takes his night hat and his

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.880
shield and his sword to bed,
and I think, we’ll that’s pretty cool,

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.709
and you can see how that makes sense, right,
and you can see how it’s an enacted reality

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:56.020
‘cause children enact or act out their reality
before they can explicitly understand it,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:02.760
just like we do, and so I’m staying there,
he wakes up, and he has, you know, one of

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.650
these fits, and then the next morning he comes
to breakfast, and I said, hey, did you have

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:11.910
any dreams last night, and he goes, yeah,
I had a dream; I said, well, tell us the dream,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.280
and there’s six adults sitting around the
table and, you know, we’re ready for this

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.789
dream, or so we think, and then he says, okay,
I was out in this field and I was surrounded

00:04:19.789 --> 00:04:25.590
by beaked dwarfs, and they came up to my knees,
and so these dwarfs they had no arms, they

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.340
just had shoulders, and powerful legs, and
they had, they were all covered with hair,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.199
and they had a cross shaved on the top of
their head, and they were all covered with

00:04:32.199 --> 00:04:37.199
grease, and everywhere I went these dwarfs
would jump up with their beaks and bite me,

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:42.779
and you’re looking at him like, that accounts
for the night terrors, right,

00:04:42.779 --> 00:04:47.270
and so then he says, yeah, and there’s more
to it too; if, if you look in the background

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:52.240
behind all the dwarfs there was a dragon way
in the background and it was puffing out fire

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.370
and smoke, and every time it puffed out fire
and smoke, a whole bunch more of these dwarfs

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.639
would get made, and you think, that’s pretty
cool, that’s a hydra story, right, remember

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:06.669
the story of the hydra, cut off one head,
two more grows, it’s one of Hercules’

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:11.779
trials, and that’s an observation about
the world, which is, you solve one problem,

00:05:11.779 --> 00:05:14.699
and like two more problems pop up, and then
you solve those and…

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:18.629
Anyways, he says, okay, well I’ve got this
dragon back there, and so this is his problem,

00:05:18.629 --> 00:05:22.599
right, he’s been eaten by beaked dwarfs,
and that’s not good, and there’s not much

00:05:22.599 --> 00:05:26.099
sense fighting them off because there’s
just more of them made every time this thing

00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:30.139
lurking in the background breathes, so I said,
what could you do about that

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:34.889
it’s like his brain was working all these
ideas around and he’d heard lots of Disney

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:39.460
stories and had lots of books read to him,
and had abstracted out a lot of information,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.809
but he hadn’t quite got it right, and it
was all seething around in his head, and I

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:47.249
just said, well, what could you do, tap, and
he went, oh I know what I could do.

00:05:47.249 --> 00:05:52.080
I could take my sword and I’d get my dad,
which is a good notion, right, ‘cause he’s

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.990
small, and then I’d jump up on the dragon
and I’d pop out both of its eyes with the

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:00.889
sword so it couldn’t see me, and then I’d
go down its throat to the box where the fire

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:05.419
came out, and then I’d carve a piece out
of the box and I’d use that as a shield,

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:10.309
and I thought, great, you really got the story,
and the story’s something like this,

00:06:10.309 --> 00:06:15.449
right: If you’re being plagued by midget
dwarfs and you wipe them out and they keep

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:18.650
multiplying, well you’re obviously aiming
at the wrong target, right, you should be

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:23.229
going to their source, so he went after the
dragon, but not only after the dragon, he

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:26.869
went right down the throat of the dragon,
which is, you know, a fairly brave thing to

00:06:26.869 --> 00:06:31.199
do, and then right to the place where the
fire, the transforming element, was being

00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:35.999
produced, and he took a piece of the device
that made the transforming element, and he

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:40.400
used it as a shield.
Okay. Well that’s really cool, and the story’s

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.659
better than that, I think, and it’s true
even, so it’s not one of those fake, he-was-dreaming-and-then-woke-up

00:06:45.659 --> 00:06:49.309
sort of stories; this actually happened; he
didn’t have any more nightmares,

00:06:49.309 --> 00:06:52.599
so when I checked with his mother, repeatedly
after that because I thought, well this is

00:06:52.599 --> 00:06:56.740
too good to be true, right, he’s got this
terrible night terror thing, he does one little

00:06:56.740 --> 00:06:59.960
mythological dream thing, and, bang, he’s,
he’s better,

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.710
but that’s the case, it is the case, he
didn’t have any more nightmares after that,

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.800
and I think that’s because he’d almost
already got it, right; he was running around

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.889
like a knight; he knew, almost, it just had
to be made a little more explicit, and not

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:18.039
even that explicit ‘cause it was still a
story. he didn’t know you should go to the

00:07:18.039 --> 00:07:22.029
source of your anxieties, right, to the thing
that plagues you the most, and you should

00:07:22.029 --> 00:07:26.539
explore that in detail until you find the
information that it contains that will protect

00:07:26.539 --> 00:07:29.389
you against it.
He couldn’t say that, but he could tell

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:35.020
the story, and he could act it out, and that
looked like it was good enough, so that’s

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:42.020
pretty cool, so he basically, you know, he
managed this, essentially, he fought the dragon

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:49.020
of chaos, popped back up, as what, as he who
can obtain victory over the dragon of chaos,

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:55.490
and that’s a pretty good story because it
says, well, if your frame of reference gets

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:01.889
blown away by something you don’t understand,
some new challenge, and you face the challenge,

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.259
at least courageously and, and humbly, which
means, you know, you’re not gonna run away,

00:08:06.259 --> 00:08:10.309
and you still have something to learn, then
you can extract something out of the battle

00:08:10.309 --> 00:08:17.309
that will enable you to withstand it.
and you think, well why should I believe that,

00:08:19.439 --> 00:08:23.460
right, and the answer to that would be, well,
don’t knock it till you try it, and the

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.759
second answer would be, that’s exactly what
we do in clinical psychotherapy all the time,

00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:31.629
and there’s endless amounts, I think, of
empirical evidence saying that, you bring

00:08:31.629 --> 00:08:35.519
someone in, they’ve got an anxiety disorder,
maybe they’re even depressed, whatever,

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:39.319
they’re running away. You say, you actually
don’t have to run away;

00:08:39.319 --> 00:08:42.930
here’s what you have to do: you have to
break the problem down into little pieces,

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:47.509
digestible pieces, and then you have to hit
it one by one, and what you’ll discover

00:08:47.509 --> 00:08:52.709
is, not that you habituate to the anxiety
because that’s a silly theory; instead what

00:08:52.709 --> 00:08:56.639
you discover is that you thought you were
the person who had to run away, but it turns

00:08:56.639 --> 00:08:59.889
out you’re not the person who has to run
away, you’re the person that can stand there

00:08:59.889 --> 00:09:04.730
while you’re anxious and learn something,
and what you most particularly learn is that

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.740
you’re the person who can stand there when
they’re anxious and learn, and if you’ve

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:14.720
learned that, you don’t have to be anxious
anymore, or even more importantly, if you’re

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.959
anxious, it doesn’t matter, it doesn’t
mean your life’s over, it just means that

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:22.160
there you are on the threshold, right, between
what you know and what you don’t know, and

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.750
you have something to learn, and you can learn
it,

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:28.540
and I think that’s what the empirical evidence
suggests too because you got Edna Foa’s

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:33.209
work with post-traumatic stress disorder victims,
primarily women who were violently raped,

00:09:33.209 --> 00:09:38.529
and Foa says, well I know you don’t like
to think about the event, and it’s no bloody

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:42.190
wonder, look what it did to you, and how terrible
it was, but if you re-live it over and over

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:46.829
and over again in your imagination, in as
much detail as possible, including all the

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:51.170
motivational and emotional details, which
she measures psycho-physiologically, you will

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:55.139
get better faster and you will stay better
longer, and her work’s well documented,

00:09:55.139 --> 00:10:00.459
and then there’s endless cases of exposure
in psychotherapy, you can certainly eliminate

00:10:00.459 --> 00:10:06.000
simple phobias within an hour, and even complex
phobias like agoraphobia, which is more like

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.050
fear of everything. It’s not an intractable
disorder

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:16.470
Imagine that throughout your whole life you
never turned away from a mistake, not even

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:22.290
once, never. So that whenever you made a mistake
that you could rectify, you did rectify it.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:26.980
Then the question would be well, what, what
exactly would you be like? Would you be suffering

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:31.329
from all your existential trouble? Would you
be vulnerable to anxiety? What would you be

00:10:31.329 --> 00:10:33.639
like?
And then I think well I only know a couple

00:10:33.639 --> 00:10:37.589
of stories like that. And the one that I told
you is the story of Solzhenitsyn. Because

00:10:37.589 --> 00:10:43.329
Solzhenitsyn, a Russian novelist sitting in
the concentration camps in the Gulag archipelago

00:10:43.329 --> 00:10:50.329
thinking, starving, this isn't so good. How
in the world did I get here? And the simple

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:55.750
story is well Stalin put you there and he
was bad, right? End of story. It's not your

00:10:55.750 --> 00:11:01.910
problem. Stalin's problem. But Solzhenitsyn
said well, that doesn't really leave me anything

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:07.389
to do right, to construe myself as a simple
victim of fate? And I do have a lot of time

00:11:07.389 --> 00:11:12.089
on my hands since I'm not really doing anything
that requires a tremendous amount of intellectual

00:11:12.089 --> 00:11:17.759
effort. Let's try a game. Let's do this. Let's
pretend that the reason that things happen

00:11:17.759 --> 00:11:23.259
to me that I don't like even terrible things
say, or that I can't tolerate, is not because

00:11:23.259 --> 00:11:29.579
I'm a victim of fate, evil cruel fate, but
because there’s something I didn't do.

00:11:29.579 --> 00:11:35.810
And so Solzhenitsyn said well, I'm gonna go
back over my whole life, right? Step by step,

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:41.480
detail by detail. And I’m gonna try to remember
every time I let something go or I didn't

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:46.350
do something I was supposed to. Not because
of some adherence to some you know, arbitrary

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:51.480
moral code, cause we don't believe in those
anyways, right? But just because I noted that

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:55.540
I can tell when I owe a debt to existence,
right?

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:59.250
So then you look at Solzhenitsyn and he says
okay, well so I spent fifteen years trying

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:04.949
to untie all the knots that I tied up in my
brain. And the consequence was, of that was

00:12:04.949 --> 00:12:08.490
first I started to notice there are some people
out there I really admired, man. They were

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.860
so tough it was unbelievable. You put them
in the worst circumstances and they didn't

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:17.079
bend an inch. They were tough. And even the
nastiest prison guards in the... well, they

00:12:17.079 --> 00:12:20.149
could kill them, that's for sure. But they
couldn't bend them. And they couldn't break

00:12:20.149 --> 00:12:23.930
them. And I really learned something from
that, right? And it's a good story. Because

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.009
he's in the worst possible circumstance. So
there's kind of no bottom past that. You don't

00:12:28.009 --> 00:12:32.040
get much worse than the Gulag prison camp,
right? That’s, it’s cold. You don't get

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.250
anything to eat and you’re being worked
to death, right? For something pointless.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:41.110
And to serve Stalin. That's, that's the bottom.
And he said even under those circumstances

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:46.009
there were still people who could, who could
thrive, who could manifest admirable qualities.

00:12:46.009 --> 00:12:51.089
He said once I figured out I was wrong, I
could actually find them and learn from them.

00:12:51.089 --> 00:12:54.529
Then he wrote this book, which you know about,
the Gulag Archipelago, which was released

00:12:54.529 --> 00:13:00.959
in the west and then circulated all through
the Soviet Union. And was undoubtedly one

00:13:00.959 --> 00:13:06.000
of the factors that contributed to the demise
of the Soviet Union. And so then you think

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:13.000
well that's pretty interesting, isn't it?
You got this one wacko, right? Russian prisoner,

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:17.569
starving to death. Tattooed. He says maybe
I had something to do with this. But he didn't

00:13:17.569 --> 00:13:21.920
mean it in some casual sort of, maybe I had
something to do with this way. He meant geez,

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.399
this is really awful. Doesn't get much worse.
Maybe it's my fault. You know, I don't know

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:31.360
how it could be. But it, after all I'm the
one that's suffering. So, maybe it was me.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.870
Maybe I could fix it. What would happen if
I did?

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:38.569
And so his conclusion was at the end. And
it's not a conclusion that he reached alone,

00:13:38.569 --> 00:13:44.149
was one person who stops lying can bring down
a tyranny. And you think that’s a metaphorical

00:13:44.149 --> 00:13:48.060
statement, right? Because you're the victim
of your own tyrannies, just as you are the

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:54.120
victim of someone else's tyrannies. And maybe
if you stop lying, construed in this manner

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:59.759
of sin of omission, right? Don't avoid anomalies
anymore. But confront them head on. Maybe

00:13:59.759 --> 00:14:04.910
if you quit lying, well then you wouldn't
be victim of tyranny. Maybe no one else would

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:11.910
be either.
The G.R.E. say, the bad exam, that’s a bad

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:18.689
thing, but it’s not the worst thing;
the worst thing is the sort of thing that

00:14:18.689 --> 00:14:24.999
knocks existentialists for a loop, right,
the worst thing is more like Ivan Karamazov’s

00:14:24.999 --> 00:14:28.589
suffering of innocent children, right, the
fact that children are tortured, or the worst

00:14:28.589 --> 00:14:33.999
thing is the fact that perfectly good people
get sick and die, and sometimes painfully,

00:14:33.999 --> 00:14:38.040
or the worst thing is there are tyrants all
over the world and they torture people for

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:42.550
no cause or maybe even just because they like
torturing people, and that’s an anomaly

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.439
of a different order, right,
it’s not just that you’re going from Point

00:14:45.439 --> 00:14:49.480
A to B and something you don’t like happens,
it’s more like thee are some aspects of

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:54.749
existence that look so terrible in and of
themselves associated with our vulnerability

00:14:54.749 --> 00:15:00.279
that just apprehending them might be enough
to knock the bottom out of your faith in any

00:15:00.279 --> 00:15:03.449
frame of reference,
and that’s a kind of Nietzschean theme,

00:15:03.449 --> 00:15:08.480
Nietzsche says: look, when you’re going
from Point A to B and something bad happens,

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:11.930
something you don’t expect, you don’t
get to where you wanted to go, that’s bad,

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.160
but what’s even worse is, you can’t have
any faith in the frame of reference that you

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:20.730
were using, ‘cause it’s been invalidated,
but what’s even worst is, you plow your

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:25.329
way through two or three frames of reference,
and then you start to develop some skepticism

00:15:25.329 --> 00:15:31.069
about frames of reference in general, right.
so I was a socialist, say, and then I was

00:15:31.069 --> 00:15:35.499
a Catholic, and, you know then I developed
some new age philosophy, and none of those

00:15:35.499 --> 00:15:40.689
really worked, and what that made me think
was, well you can’t trust socialism, you

00:15:40.689 --> 00:15:45.160
can’t trust Christianity, and those new
age people are certainly out to lunch, maybe

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:50.230
you can’t trust any frames of reference,
and that’s a really devastating discovery,

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:55.189
and Nietzsche associated that with the death
of God, right, it’s like, no frames of reference

00:15:55.189 --> 00:15:58.970
work, and then you have the problem that,
well, without a frame of reference life is

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:04.829
chaos, and chaos is intolerable, and therefore
logically life is intolerable,

00:16:04.829 --> 00:16:10.110
and I tried to make a case for you then that’s
kind of a side case, which was, people protect

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:13.860
their ideologies ‘cause they don’t want
to lose their frames of reference, they don’t

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:20.379
want to fall into chaos, but then there’s
this additional problem, which is that you

00:16:20.379 --> 00:16:26.610
can develop a kind of deep cynicism about
life in a secondary manner, which is like

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:32.120
constant loss of faith.
maybe what you conclude under those conditions,

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:39.120
like the aggressive child concludes, is that,
fundamentally, I’m not to be trusted, you’re

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:43.930
not to be trusted, society is not to be trusted,
and maybe the structure of the world as a

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:50.930
whole isn’t to be trusted, and, therefore,
logically, you’re more or less obligated

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:57.079
to work against it,
and so then you have a nice sub-story for

00:16:57.079 --> 00:17:03.529
the propagation of evil, which is, well, we
like to have our ideological frames of reference

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:07.780
retained, and that gives us ample reason to
squash anyone that’s different, but then

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:11.820
there’s this additional reason, which is,
when you get right down to it, things are

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:16.870
pretty, bloody awful, and maybe the sensible
thing to do is to just work for the in annihilation

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:21.040
of things, and I think we’ve had endless
examples of people who did precisely that

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.400
in the 20th Century and almost got away with
it,

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:30.400
in case you’re tempted not to take this
sufficiently seriously, right, we know that

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:36.420
Stalin, in all likelihood,, who I think you
could make a case for being if not the most

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:40.500
evil man that ever lived, certainly the most
evil man that lived this century, and that’s

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:44.240
really a high honor, right, ‘cause he was
up against some really top contenders.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:49.760
We know, as a consequence of recently-released
KGB documents, that he was probably gearing

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.300
up to start the Third World War, and not one
of these little half-rate, you know, little

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:59.570
local Third World Wars, we are talking about
the whole H bomb exchange thing designed to

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:03.590
eradicate, you know, the U.S. for sure, but
also the Soviet Union,

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:08.340
and, well, mere territoriality isn’t enough
to account for that, but then maybe you can

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:12.670
see Stalin’s point, right, like Tolstoy
can see it, you know, if life is really so

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:18.130
awful at bottom, which there are perspectives
from which that certainly seems to be the

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:24.330
case, then why bother having it around at
all.

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:31.330
Well, you know, that’s a pretty dismal perspective,
so that’s a real anomaly, right, that’s

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.700
not one of these little second-rate you’ll-get-over-it-in-a-month-or-two
anomalies,

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:41.650
this is the sort of anomaly that’s laid
out in Genesis, where Adam and Eve discover

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:46.330
that they’re mortal, vulnerable, they’re
gonna die, that really takes the shine off

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:51.490
existence out of paradise they go, they wander
around the planet for the rest of history,

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:55.080
you know, working themselves to death and
being miserable and killing each other, and

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.540
that’s basically the story that’s laid
out in the Old Testament, and viewed from

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:04.010
that perspective, well, it’s not precisely
an empirical description of the big bang say,

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:08.930
but it’s not a bad description of the nature
of human existence, and it’s pretty dismal

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:15.930
There’s an essential symbolic relationship
between the ingestion of food and its transformative

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:25.090
capacities and the, and the ingestion of ideas,
and their transformative capacity.

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:29.150
and what happens when Adam and Eve eat this
fruit, which they’re not supposed to eat,

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:35.020
is that they learn that they’re going to
die, and that screws up Paradise,

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:39.510
and in case you just think I’m making this
up, which would be, you know, kind of annoying,

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:44.080
then you want to look at this picture, which
is from the 14th Century, and it’s a really

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:49.630
remarkable picture, so what you’ve got in
the middle here is the, the tree of the fruit

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:55.010
of the knowledge of good and evil, and you’ve
got Eve over here, and you’ve got the Church

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:59.550
here, and what you see happening, you got
to look really carefully at this tree ‘cause

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:03.270
the first thing you see is it’s got this
snake wrapped around it, this agent of transformation,

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:10.270
right, who’s associated with Satan, and
then up in the branches you have apples and

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:17.760
you have skulls, and then if you look at Eve
here, she’s got grapes here, and a skull

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:23.220
in her hand, and what this artist is trying
to indicate is that there’s this tight relationship

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:30.220
between Eve tempting Adam towards higher knowledge
and delivering him death.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.020
so that’s a pretty dismal story, and all
the people over here on the left side, all

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:40.090
these unhappy people, are the people who are
living in chaos and misery as a consequence

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:45.540
of being, of having their vulnerability revealed
to them, and that’s the negative side of

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:47.750
the story,
but then there’s the positive side of the

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:51.820
story over here, and it’s, it’s just as
complicated, and that’s partly why it’s

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:56.460
expressed in imagistic form,
so you’ve got the Church here, symbolized

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:01.610
at least in part as Mary, and she’s handing
out something too, and if you look at those

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:07.110
they’re little round circles with crosses
on them, and what those are, are hosts, hosts,

00:21:07.110 --> 00:21:14.110
they’re the symbols of transformation, particularly
in Catholicism; now that’s a very complicated

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:17.930
idea, and this is the idea, it’s something
like this:

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:22.890
At Christ’s last supper before he was crucified,
he told his disciples that they were going

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:28.760
to have to ingest him, right, so they gave
them wine and bread, and the wine was blood

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:34.700
and the bread was flesh, and what’s the
idea, what does it mean to incorporate someone?

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:40.090
It means to embody them, that’s what it
means, and this, this imagistic ritualistic

00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:46.480
process, is the notion that in order to attain
redemption, it’s necessary to embody the

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:49.850
hero, and that’s kind of what this picture
is trying to portray,

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:54.680
it says, okay, well you got this death apple
over on the left-hand side, and that’s not

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:59.750
so good, and you need an antidote to it, and
the antidote is whatever this represents,

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:04.260
whatever this represents, and you see up in
the tree here there’s all these hosts hanging.

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:08.100
Now the hosts are representative of Christ,
and for complicated reasons they’re made

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:12.410
out of wheat, say, and partly the reason they’re
made out of wheat is because if you look at

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:19.410
hero Gods prior to Christianity, you see that
wheat was often conceptualized as a dying

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:23.970
and redeemed God, right, because it would
die in the Winter and then be re-born in the

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:29.770
Spring, just like all plants are, and the
notion of the dying and redeeming, the dying

00:22:29.770 --> 00:22:36.470
and resurrecting hero, was kind of, what would
you say, layered on top of that older agrarian

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:39.800
idea, and all mixed together, and it sort
of popped out in this idea of the, of the

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:43.130
host,
and so the idea here is that whatever ails

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:49.840
human beings, which is their knowledge of
vulnerability and death, can be rectified

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:54.300
by their incorporation of whatever this symbol
represents,

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:58.870
and so then you might ask, well what exactly
does that symbol represent, and, of course,

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:04.200
the standard Christian answers to that, and
the extended Christian answers are, well it

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:09.350
represents your faith in Christ, say, but
that’s not a very useful answer, all things

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:13.910
considered, so let’s look at it in a little
bit more complicated way.

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:20.310
It’s not a useful answer I think because
it’s too sectarian, right, it excludes many,

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:24.390
many people, this notion, and there’s a
whole formalism that you have to buy into

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:29.560
to even get access to what that story means,
and it’s an unfortunate formalism because

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:33.750
first of all I think it’s more appropriate
to an earlier time and place, and second of

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:40.590
all because I think we’re actually sophisticated
enough now intellectually, psychologically,

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.330
to actually start to understand what some
of these stories mean,

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:47.320
and since we have reasonably well-developed
brains, and we might as well use them, it

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:51.850
would be better if they were on our side,
so to speak, than constantly conspiring to

00:23:51.850 --> 00:23:58.850
undermine our faith,
let's look at what a person is like. And a

00:24:01.670 --> 00:24:06.530
person is sort of just as complicated as a,
an object, which is not that surprising cause

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:11.860
there's an aspect of us that is object like,
right? Our objective being.

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:18.510
And we know people are unbelievably complicated.
They have they have nervous systems that have

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:23.460
more connections in them than there are subatomic
particles in the universe, just for starters.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:27.280
And so that means that when you're looking
at another person, you're looking at something

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:32.530
that's more complicated than anything else
that exists anywhere, including the sum total

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:38.200
of everything that exists everywhere, except
other people. More complex than everything.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:42.010
And then you have to understand too that just
because you don't think of yourself that way

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:46.900
doesn’t mean you're not that way.
It just means that you're conscious mind,

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:53.270
your rational mind say isn't sophisticated
enough to actually completely model who or

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:58.400
what you are. And that's obvious because that's
why we study ourselves. We don't know who

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.840
we are. We're trying to figure it out. We've
been trying to figure it out ever since we

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:06.320
woke some thousands of years ago. We don't
know when

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.990
And you think well if you look at people,
well you know, there's the kind of obvious

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.820
level that you see people at, the self level,
which is the privileged level of analysis

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:19.990
for the west. But you’re a member of a family.
And if I said well are you more yourself or

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:24.650
your family, you might say well, most of the
time I think I'm more myself, but I might

00:25:24.650 --> 00:25:30.000
be willing to sacrifice my life for my child's.
In which case I would say well, then you’re

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.070
just as much your child as you are you. Or
maybe you’re even more your child. And what

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:39.190
about your family? Well that's a tough question
too. And then what about your cultural group?

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:43.370
Well you say no, it's me, not my cultural
group. But then I'd say well what if there's

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:48.890
a war? Is it you or your cultural group? And
then you’ll say oh it's my cultural group.

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:55.890
And then you see as well, well at this level
of analysis are you your biological group?

00:25:56.120 --> 00:26:01.230
Is that what you identify with, the biosphere
say? Say well no, not generally. But there's

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:06.500
a lot of environmentalists out there. And
they say well what we should primarily be

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:11.280
concerned with is the global health of the
planet because our survival depends on that.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:15.510
We're as much that as we are the self. And
you might not agree with that. And I suspect

00:26:15.510 --> 00:26:20.270
that most of the time there's screwy reasons
for proposing such a thing. But on the other

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:25.110
hand, a case can be made.
I mean we know that you can undermine your

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:29.270
ecosystems. It happened in Spain. They let,
four hundred years ago they let the sheep

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:33.860
eat everything. And so Spain turned into a
desert. Doesn't seem like a particularly wise

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:37.560
move.
And then you think well below the phenomenological

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:42.160
level there's all these sub elements of you,
your physiological structure, your cellular

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:49.160
structure, your atomic structure. To infinite
investigation. Absolutely complex. You'll

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:54.010
never exhaust it if you investigate it. And
it's perfectly reasonable to presuppose that

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:55.010
you're all these things

00:26:55.010 --> 00:27:01.950
How does it change the world if you stop thinking
about it as made of objects? But, but instead

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:05.410
made of your own experience. And how does
it change the world if you think you have

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:10.820
an ethical relationship with that experience
that, that's a primary fact, not some secondary

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:15.960
derivative. So primary a fact that you can't
even look at the world except through an ethical

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:21.170
lens, primary fact. What is, how does that
change the way you conceptualize yourself

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:26.100
in relationship to the world? I don't know.

